  "narration": {
    "title": "Alonso's Four-Homer Week, and a Few Cold Spells",
    "intro": "The week's climbers hit, and the fallers mostly went quiet at the plate. Pete Alonso homered four times to reach 40, while a couple of good bats slipped a few points. Nobody's arm lost ground, which is what late September looks like.",
    "risers_lead": "Pete Alonso led the home-run climbers with four to hit 41, matching Ben Rice, who added three to reach the same number. CJ Abrams and Brandon Lowe each chipped in three, keeping the middle of the leaderboard busy.",
    "pfallers_lead": "Nothing to report here: no strong arm gave back form this week, which tracks with a settled rotation picture heading into October.",
    "hweek_lead": "Angel Mart\u00ednez had the loudest bat of the week, 8-for-17 with three homers for a 1.589 OPS, just ahead of Zack Gelof's four-homer, eight-RBI stretch in Oakland. Mookie Betts and Will Smith both scorched it for the Dodgers.",
    "fallers_lead": "The cold spells were modest: Luis Garc\u00eda Jr. went 2-for-19 over five games and Jordan Walker 2-for-22, each shaving about twenty points of season OPS. Small dents, not slides.",
    "colors_lead": "One tier move worth noting: James Wood dropped from elite to above-average, the lone recolor on the board this week."
  }
